# Lesson 3, new exercise: dummy's clubs are missing the ace and king. Lose both early, while you still hold stoppers.
title: Two honors to force out
id: L03-15
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.2, 3.4, 3.5
student: S
board: 47
dealer: S
vulnerable: NS

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  A72    Q82    54     QJT98
  E  53     J976   QJ9    AK76
  S  K64    AK5    AK72   432
  W  QJT98  T43    T863   5

auction:
  S 1NT*   = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 17 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 2NT    = 8-9 points, balanced: invites game
  E Pass
  S 3NT*   = Accepts: a maximum 1NT
     key: 17 is the top of your range. Accept the invitation.
     not Pass: Partner invited you to bid on with the top of your range. Did you notice that 17 is the top?
     not *: Partner's 2NT asks: are you at the top of your 15-17?
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner invited with 2NT. With 17, the top of your
  range, you accepted. You are declarer, because you bid no-trump first, and
  so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 10
  spades: 2 = The ace and king. One in each hand, to stop the spades twice.
  hearts: 3 = The ace, king and queen.
  diamonds: 2 = The ace and king.
  clubs: 3 = Three, once the ace and king are gone.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit.
  remind: Dummy's clubs are missing the ace and the king. How many tricks will they give once both are gone?
play:
  1   W SQ  N S2  E S3  S SK
      note: Seven winners on top: two spades, three hearts and two diamonds. Dummy's clubs give three more, but only after the ace and the king are gone. You'll lose the lead twice, so keep a spade stopper in each hand: win with the king in your hand.
  2   S C2  W C5  N CQ  E CA
      note: Start on clubs right away.
  3   E S5  S S4  W ST  N SA
      note: East returns partner's spades. Dummy's ace stops them a second time.
  4   N CJ  E CK  S C3  W H3
      note: Keep going: force out the king. If East had held it back, dummy's ♥Q would still be a way back to the clubs later.
  5   E DQ  S DA  W D3  N D4
      note: East is out of spades and tries diamonds. You still have the ♦A.
  6   S C4  W H4  N CT  E C6
      note: Your last club takes you to dummy's winners.
  7   N C9  E C7  S D2  W S8
      note: Keep cashing dummy's clubs.
  8   N C8  E H6  S S6  W S9
      note: The last club.
  9   N HQ  E H7  S H5  W HT
      note: Now the hearts: dummy's queen first, while you're in dummy.
  10  N H8  E H9  S HK  W D6
      note: Then over to your king...
  11  S DK  W D8  N D5  E D9
      note: ...and your other winners.
  12  S HA  W DT  N H2  E HJ
  13  S D7  W SJ  N S7  E DJ
      note: 10 tricks: 3NT made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: count seven winners on top, then force out the ♣A and ♣K to set up
  dummy's clubs.

  You had to give up the lead twice, and you had a spade stopper ready each
  time. West's spades were set up by the end, but West never got in to take
  them.
